{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"enqueue","timestamp":"2026-05-12T03:55:45.484Z","sessionId":"ef324e8c-8832-4837-8183-bec6f1e417f7","content":"<task-notification>\n<task-id>a5bdc578786822b22</task-id>\n<tool-use-id>toolu_012cqz1XU1bSrLG8YMY2xGia</tool-use-id>\n<output-file>/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-peytonmontei-Documents-entire-devenv-entireio-cli--worktrees-review-fix-2/ef324e8c-8832-4837-8183-bec6f1e417f7/tasks/a5bdc578786822b22.output</output-file>\n<status>completed</status>\n<summary>Agent \"Silent failure scan of PR 1175\" completed</summary>\n<result>I have enough context. Let me compile the findings now.\n\n---\n\n# PR #1175 Error Handling Review\n\n## Summary verdict\n\nThis PR is mostly defensible. The new context composer (`review_context.go`) is genuinely best-effort enrichment of an agent prompt — the design intent is \"if context can't be gathered, the review still runs\" — and the implementation honours that with consistent `logging.Debug` traces on every error path. The committed-checkpoint section and in-progress section ARE independent, so a failure in one does not nuke the other. `scope.go` fails loud on `--base`, exits early with a meaningful error, and never panics. `cmd.go` validates `--base` before agent spawn (verified by `TestReviewCommandSmoke_BadBaseRefErrorsBeforeAgentSpawn`).\n\nThat said, there are several issues worth raising. None are CRITICAL. Two are HIGH (one classifier ambiguity that legitimately could hide bugs, one inconsistent `cmd.SilenceUsage` pattern), and the rest are MEDIUM.\n\n---\n\n## Findings\n\n### 1. MEDIUM — `formatReviewSessionLine` cannot distinguish \"no prompt yet\" from \"I/O error on prompt.txt\"\n**Location:** `/Users/peytonmontei/Documents/entire/devenv/entireio/cli/.worktrees/review-fix-2/cmd/entire/cli/review_context.go:244-253`\n\n```go\nraw, err := os.ReadFile(promptPath)\nif err != nil {\n    return \"\"\n}\n```\n\nThe PR description claims \"Sessions whose prompt.txt is missing or empty are skipped silently.\" That phrasing implies `os.IsNotExist(err)` and \"empty content\" — two well-defined, expected, optional-input cases. But the code's actual behaviour is \"any `os.ReadFile` error, including permission-denied, EIO, EACCES, ELOOP (symlink loops), and ENAMETOOLONG, is silently swallowed and the session disappears from the context block.\" StateStore.List already swallows corrupt-JSON state files at `session/state.go:548`, so the silent-skip chain runs two layers deep with no operator trace.\n\n**Hidden errors:**\n- Permission-denied on `.entire/metadata/&lt;session&gt;/prompt.txt` (umask / chmod drift after `entire enable`)\n- `EACCES` on parent directory traversal\n- `EIO` (filesystem corruption)\n- A genuinely zero-byte `prompt.txt` (which IS a real bug — the lifecycle hook failed to append for every turn this session, but the file got created)\n\n**User impact:** A user who explicitly knows they have an active session in this worktree will see no \"In-progress session context\" block at all and have no way to know why. There's no debug line to grep for.\n\n**Recommendation:** Split the cases. `os.IsNotExist(err)` and empty content → silent skip (these ARE expected for a fresh session that hasn't taken a turn yet). Any other I/O error → `logging.Debug(ctx, \"review session context: read prompt\", slog.String(\"session_id\", st.SessionID), slog.String(\"error\", err.Error()))` then skip. Pass `ctx` into `formatReviewSessionLine` to enable this.\n\n```go\nfunc formatReviewSessionLine(ctx context.Context, worktreeRoot string, st *session.State) string {\n    promptPath := filepath.Join(worktreeRoot, paths.SessionMetadataDirFromSessionID(st.SessionID), paths.PromptFileName)\n    raw, err := os.ReadFile(promptPath)\n    switch {\n    case err == nil:\n        // fall through\n    case errors.Is(err, fs.ErrNotExist):\n        return \"\" // expected: session hasn't completed a turn yet\n    default:\n        logging.Debug(ctx, \"review session context: read prompt failed\",\n            slog.String(\"session_id\", st.SessionID),\n            slog.String(\"path\", promptPath),\n            slog.String(\"error\", err.Error()))\n        return \"\"\n    }\n    // ...\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n### 2. MEDIUM — `canonicalisePath` silently swallows non-ENOENT errors from `EvalSymlinks`\n**Location:** `/Users/peytonmontei/Documents/entire/devenv/entireio/cli/.worktrees/review-fix-2/cmd/entire/cli/review_context.go:232-240`\n\n```go\nif resolved, err := filepath.EvalSymlinks(p); err == nil {\n    return resolved\n}\nreturn p\n```\n\nThe doc comment correctly notes the ENOENT case (\"path doesn't exist yet\"). But `EvalSymlinks` also returns errors for symlink loops, EACCES on intermediate components, and ENAMETOOLONG — none of which the function distinguishes from ENOENT. When the comparison is symmetric (both sides fall back to raw), this is usually harmless. But it can produce subtle false negatives: if the on-disk state file stores a `/private/var/...` form AND `EvalSymlinks` on the caller's worktree path fails with EACCES while the state path resolves cleanly, the two sides compare unequal and the session is silently dropped.\n\n**Hidden errors:** EACCES on a parent, symlink loop, ENAMETOOLONG.\n\n**Recommendation:** Either swap to debug-logged switch (similar to finding 1), or document explicitly that the fallback chain is \"any error → raw path comparison; comparison is symmetric so worst case both sides degrade together.\" The current `Falls back to p itself when EvalSymlinks fails (e.g., the path doesn't exist yet)` comment misleads — `(e.g., ...)` reads as \"this is the only case\" when in fact several other error classes also hit the fallback.\n\n---\n\n### 3. MEDIUM — Empty `prompt.txt` content is silently treated as \"no prompt\", masking a lifecycle-hook bug\n**Location:** `/Users/peytonmontei/Documents/entire/devenv/entireio/cli/.worktrees/review-fix-2/cmd/entire/cli/review_context.go:250-253`\n\n```go\npromptText := reviewPromptText(string(raw))\nif promptText == \"\" {\n    return \"\"\n}\n```\n\nIf `prompt.txt` exists but is zero bytes (or contains only whitespace/separators), the function returns `\"\"` and the session is dropped from the context block with no trace. A zero-byte `prompt.txt` for an existing session — i.e., the state.json says this session has done N turns — would mean the lifecycle hook silently failed to append at every prompt boundary. That is a real bug class (`lifecycle.go:294-310` writes to the file on every turn).\n\n**Hidden errors:** lifecycle-hook write failures (the hook silently no-ops on disk-full, the file got truncated by an external process, an editor accidentally cleared it).\n\n**Recommendation:** When `raw` is non-empty but `reviewPromptText` returns `\"\"`, log at debug level. Distinguish from the zero-byte case (which is itself worth a debug line, given the state file says turns happened).\n\n---\n\n### 4. HIGH — `scope.go` fallback chain returns a generic error when ALL refs are missing\n**Location:** `/Users/peytonmontei/Documents/entire/devenv/entireio/cli/.worktrees/review-fix-2/cmd/entire/cli/review/scope.go:163-171`\n\n```go\nreturn \"\", errors.New(\"no suitable ancestor branch found; configure a base ref explicitly\")\n```\n\nThis is the failure mode that fires when a freshly-cloned shallow repo or a brand-new local-only repo has none of `origin/HEAD origin/main origin/master main master`. The message says \"configure a base ref explicitly\" but never mentions HOW (the `--base` flag), and never names the chain it tried. The user sees this through `runReview → detectScope → ComputeScopeStats` wrapping: the final stderr will be `detect scope base ref: no suitable ancestor branch found; configure a base ref explicitly` — opaque to anyone who didn't read the source.\n\n**Hidden errors:** none — but the error message itself is so terse it provides no actionable guidance. The CLAUDE.md rule \"user-facing error messages must explain what