nchOffMain` at `scope_test.go:153` checks the count is 3 when the branch is a fast-forward of main — that passes with both `base..HEAD` and `base...HEAD`. To force a divergence, the test must commit on `main` *after* the branch point.\n\n**What's missing:** a test where `main` advances after `feat/x` diverges. With two-dot, files changed only on main would be counted; with three-dot, only files unique to `feat/x` are counted. Without this test, a future contributor could revert the dot count thinking it's equivalent and the banner stat would silently regress. Bug-class: numeric over-count in a user-visible banner.\n\n### 2. No test for `detectScopeBaseRef` fallback chain ordering — rating 7-8\n\n`scope.go:163-171` declares the precedence `origin/HEAD → origin/main → origin/master → main → master`. Test coverage:\n\n- `TestDetectScopeBaseRef_BranchOffMain` exercises only the \"main exists\" leg (everything past the first hit is untested).\n- `TestDetectScopeBaseRef_NoSuitableAncestor` at `scope_test.go:319` verifies the all-empty case errors.\n\n**What's missing:**\n- A test where `master` exists but `main` does not (verifies master fallback works).\n- A test where `origin/HEAD` symbolic ref is set, distinct from `origin/main` — verifies `origin/HEAD` wins over `origin/main` when both exist and point to different branches. This is the most likely real-world precedence-disagreement scenario and is the *first* element in the chain.\n- A test where `origin/main` exists but `origin/HEAD` does not — common on remotes where `git remote set-head` was never run.\n\nWithout these, the `fallbackScopeRef` precedence could be silently reordered and only fixed-branch-name tests would catch the most-common case.\n\n### 3. `detectScope` failure on `git.PlainOpen` is untested — rating 7\n\n`cmd/entire/cli/review/cmd.go:433-459` has a dedicated branch when `PlainOpen` fails *with* a `--base` override (`fmt.Errorf(\"--base %q given but cannot open repository...\")` at line 442). The `TestReviewCommandSmoke_BadBaseRefErrorsBeforeAgentSpawn` test at `review_context_test.go:326` exercises the *invalid ref* path, not the *unopenable repo* path.\n\n**What's missing:** a test that points the command at a non-repo directory with `--base` set, asserting the specific PlainOpen error path returns the loud \"--base given but cannot open repository\" message. The current smoke test would pass even if line 441-443 were deleted.\n\nSeverity is moderate (this path is defense-in-depth), but the existing comment explicitly calls out a consistency property (\"inconsistent with the ComputeScopeStats error path\") that has no enforcement.\n\n## Important Improvements (5-7)\n\n### 4. In-progress session filter: missing-state-file and missing-prompt-file paths — rating 5\n\n`reviewSessionContext` (`review_context.go:178-227`) silently skips sessions whose `prompt.txt` is unreadable (`formatReviewSessionLine` at line 247-249) and returns `\"\"` if `store.List` fails. Coverage:\n\n- `TestReviewSessionContext_SkipsSessionsOutsideScope` covers the four explicit exclusion criteria (`FullyCondensed`, `WrongWorktree`, `WrongBaseCommit`, `KindAgentReview`).\n\n**Gaps:**\n- No test for \"state file present, prompt.txt missing on disk\" — `formatReviewSessionLine` returns `\"\"` here. A regression that started rendering bare lines (`&lt;id&gt; &lt;agent&gt;` with no prompt) wouldn't fail any test.\n- No test for \"state file present, prompt.txt empty after trimming\" — same exit path.\n- No test for \"two qualifying sessions both produce lines\" — verifies the loop accumulates rather than only returning the first.\n\nThese are quality-of-life rather than correctness-critical, but the silent-skip semantic is exactly the kind of behavior that drifts.\n\n### 5. Symlink canonicalization branch on macOS — rating 5\n\n`canonicalisePath` at `review_context.go:232-240` exists specifically to handle `/var → /private/var` symlinks on macOS. `TestReviewSessionContext_SkipsSessionsOutside